### 2. Points on a sunny line  

The reduction of the slope to a coprime fraction \(\frac pq\) with \(p\neq0,\;p\neq -q\) is valid.  
The deduction that integer points on the line have \(x\)-coordinates differing by multiples of \(q\) and that the sum \(x+y\) changes by \((p+q)t\) is correct.  

From this the bound (3) follows: the possible values of \(x+y\) lie in the interval \([2,n+1]\), so the number of admissible values is at most \(\bigl\lfloor\frac{n-1}{|p+q|}\bigr\rfloor+1\). The inequality \(\le\bigl\lfloor\frac{n}{|p+q|}\bigr\rfloor+1\) is a harmless weakening.  

