The solution argues that because each sunny line can occupy at most one point per column, having \(k\ge4\) would “force at least four columns to rely on sunny lines” and then runs out of vertical lines.  
*Critical Error:* This reasoning only examines the three right‑most columns and ignores the general counting condition. For many values of \(n\) (e.g. \(n=5\)) one can construct a configuration with \(k=4\): take the vertical line \(x=1\) (covering the five points of column 1) and four sunny lines, each passing through a distinct point of the remaining columns. This satisfies all requirements, contradicting the claim that \(k\ge4\) is impossible. Hence the impossibility proof is invalid.

